---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is DNS rebinding still a threat?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes, especially against IoT devices and internal services that lack host-header validation. Browsers have improved, but the attack surface persists."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How is this different from DNS spoofing?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Spoofing forges answers. Rebinding uses legitimate answers that change over time: the DNS is working correctly, the attack exploits the time gap."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does HTTPS prevent rebinding?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "It complicates it (the attacker needs a valid certificate for their domain that rebinding preserves), but does not eliminate the underlying issue for internal HTTP services."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I prevent rebinding on my own devices?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Set your resolver to filter rebind attempts (dnsmasq: stop-dns-rebind). Most modern routers and Pi-hole include this."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why doesn’t the same-origin policy prevent this?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The browser judges origin by domain NAME, not IP. attacker.com resolving to 192.168.1.1 is still attacker.com to the browser."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the DNS TTL requirement for rebinding?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The attacker needs a very low TTL (0 or 1) so the browser re-queries quickly. Legitimate domains should never use TTL 0."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DNS Rebinding Attacks Explained"
  description="DNS rebinding explained: how attackers bypass the same-origin policy using DNS to access internal networks, and how to prevent it."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["DNS Security", "/dns-security/"], ["DNS Rebinding"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns-security/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Security</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DNS Rebinding</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS Rebinding Explained</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DNS rebinding exploits the time gap between DNS resolution and browser security checks. The attacker’s domain first resolves to their own server (to plant malicious JavaScript), then rebinds to an internal IP (to access devices on your network) using the browser’s trust.</p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-attack-sequence">The attack sequence</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-attackers-target">What attackers target</a></li>
        <li><a href="#defenses">Defenses</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dns-security-cluster">DNS Security Cluster</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="the-attack-sequence">The attack sequence</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>The victim visits attacker.com, which resolves to the attacker’s server.</li><li>The page loads JavaScript that keeps running in the victim’s browser.</li><li>The DNS TTL expires; the page re-queries attacker.com.</li><li>This time the DNS answer is 192.168.1.1 (the victim’s router).</li><li>The JavaScript can now read responses from 192.168.1.1 because the browser thinks it’s still same-origin (same domain name).</li></ol>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="what-attackers-target">What attackers target</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>Router admin panels (change DNS, open ports, install firmware).</li><li>IoT devices (cameras, thermostats) with unauthenticated APIs.</li><li>Development servers (localhost:3000, :8080) running on the victim’s machine.</li><li>Enterprise applications on internal networks accessible from the employee’s browser.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="defenses">Defenses</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Browser-level:</strong> modern browsers pin the resolved IP for the page’s lifetime (defense in depth).</li><li><strong class="text-ink">DNS-level:</strong> your resolver can filter rebinding attempts (answer A records for public domains only with public IPs).</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Application-level:</strong> validate the Host header on internal services; reject requests for external domain names.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Router-level:</strong> don’t expose admin interfaces on the LAN without authentication.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is DNS rebinding still a threat?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, especially against IoT devices and internal services that lack host-header validation. Browsers have improved, but the attack surface persists.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How is this different from DNS spoofing?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Spoofing forges answers. Rebinding uses legitimate answers that change over time: the DNS is working correctly, the attack exploits the time gap.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does HTTPS prevent rebinding?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">It complicates it (the attacker needs a valid certificate for their domain that rebinding preserves), but does not eliminate the underlying issue for internal HTTP services.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I prevent rebinding on my own devices?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Set your resolver to filter rebind attempts (dnsmasq: stop-dns-rebind). Most modern routers and Pi-hole include this.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why doesn’t the same-origin policy prevent this?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The browser judges origin by domain NAME, not IP. attacker.com resolving to 192.168.1.1 is still attacker.com to the browser.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is the DNS TTL requirement for rebinding?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The attacker needs a very low TTL (0 or 1) so the browser re-queries quickly. Legitimate domains should never use TTL 0.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="dns-security-cluster">DNS Security Cluster</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Every attack type and its defense, cross-referenced:</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2">
<li><a href="/dns-hijacking/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS Hijacking</a></li>
<li><a href="/dns-cache-poisoning/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS Cache Poisoning</a></li>
<li><a href="/dns-amplification-attack/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS Amplification Attacks</a></li>
<li><a href="/dns-tunneling/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS Tunneling</a></li>
<li><a href="/what-is-dnssec/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNSSEC: the cryptographic defense</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/what-is-dnssec/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS security →</a></li><li><a href="/public-vs-private-ip/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Public vs private IP →</a></li><li><a href="/how-dns-works/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">How DNS works →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
